WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.790
- Let's call in my first guest, everybody

00:00:01.790 --> 00:00:04.880
no matter the weather, he
always puts a smile on our face.

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:07.170
Like you I'm smiling right
now just thinking about him.

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:08.050
I love them.

00:00:08.050 --> 00:00:10.650
You start every morning with
him on the Today show on NBC.

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:12.338
Let's say hello to Al Roker!

00:00:12.338 --> 00:00:14.921
(upbeat music)

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:18.590
Yeah. You're feeling it.

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:19.423
You're feeling it.

00:00:19.423 --> 00:00:20.290
Little body roll?

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:21.123
Yeah (laughs).

00:00:22.337 --> 00:00:23.730
But you and Jenny have
something in common.

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:26.790
You also wrote some novels,
right? Which I didn't know.

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:29.040
- Yeah. I wrote three murder mysteries.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:31.160
I've written 13 books in all,

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:34.987
but I wrote three murder mysteries.

00:00:34.987 --> 00:00:36.947
"The Morning Show Murders",
"The Talk Show Murders",

00:00:36.947 --> 00:00:38.370
"The Late Night Show Murders".

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:42.063
And they star this guy
named Billy Blessing.

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:45.250
I've always loved murder mysteries

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:47.030
and they say, "Write what you know."

00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:49.060
So the first one was "The
Morning Show Murders".

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:54.040
So somebody on a morning
news program gets murdered

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:57.640
and Billy Blessing
helped solve the murder.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:00:58.540
- So this is why I didn't know.

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:00.240
So my mother and like a lot of my friends

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:01.290
are really into a murder mysteries.

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:02.920
I don't think I've ever read one.

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:04.110
So now I'm gonna do it.

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:05.420
'Cause I didn't know this about you

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:08.170
and now I'm like really curious
to know about Billy Blessing

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:09.003
and who did it.

00:01:09.003 --> 00:01:10.290
Now I wanna know who done it.

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:12.180
But who is Billy Blessing?

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:13.750
Did you, like I was telling Jenny

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:16.040
like some characters are kinda
inspired from my real life,

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:19.690
like Muses so is Billy Blessing,
is he based off anyone?

00:01:19.690 --> 00:01:23.290
- Well, he happens to be a chef.

00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:27.440
He's bald, he's chunky, he's black.

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:29.738
And he works on a morning show.

00:01:29.738 --> 00:01:30.571
Hmm.

00:01:30.571 --> 00:01:33.154
(Kelly laughs)

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:37.773
But the only differences is
he's really good looking.

00:01:37.773 --> 00:01:41.150
So that's where we part in company.

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:42.166
- Whatever.

00:01:42.166 --> 00:01:45.420
Since I saw you last I was sorry to hear

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:47.020
of your prostate cancer diagnosis.

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:48.500
So how are you feeling?

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:51.230
- You know, Kelly? I really feel good.

00:01:51.230 --> 00:01:54.100
I was very fortunate. We caught it early.

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:57.140
It was an aggressive
form of prostate cancer

00:01:57.140 --> 00:01:59.716
but we caught it very early.

00:01:59.716 --> 00:02:02.700
I had a great surgeon from
Memorial Sloan Kettering,

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:04.180
Dr. Vincent Laudone.

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:08.270
And he feels we've got it all.
I'm waiting for blood work.

00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:13.270
And once my PSA level is
below like 0.05 that means

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:17.610
there's no prostate cancer at this moment.

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:19.210
And what I'm gonna be
doing is getting tested

00:02:19.210 --> 00:02:21.830
every six months for the next five years.

00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:23.920
And if that turns out well

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:26.590
then it's once a year testing
for the rest of my life.

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:29.430
But you know, I went public with this

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:32.830
because I did want
people, it's a simple test

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:36.860
but a lot of guys, you know, we're wimpy.

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:39.070
We don't like going to the doctor.

00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:42.290
And this is an easy test to do.

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:45.330
I mean, there's obviously the digital exam

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:48.640
where we all remember from
Fletch with Chevy, Chevy Chase.

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:49.991
Moon river!

00:02:49.991 --> 00:02:50.910
(Kelly laughs)

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:52.080
- I love you (indistinct).

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:56.003
- That is in fact the gold standard.

00:02:57.140 --> 00:03:00.280
And especially for men of
color, African-American men

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:05.280
50% more likely to be
diagnosed with prostate cancer

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:08.640
and twice as likely to die from it.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:09.950
There are a number of factors in that.

00:03:09.950 --> 00:03:12.550
So I just want people to get checked

00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:13.930
get their prostate checked.

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:16.390
And especially if you're a man of color

00:03:16.390 --> 00:03:18.330
get your prostate checked.

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:20.240
It's not that big a deal.

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:22.920
And it can make a big
deal because the fact is

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:24.163
if you live long enough,

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:29.500
my doctor told me you won't
die from prostate cancer

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:32.113
but you probably die with prostate cancer,

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:36.370
but making sure you get
checked, you can manage it.

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:37.820
- You have to. It's your body.

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:39.240
It's your temple. You
gotta take care of it.

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:41.250
And you gotta keep
checking up on yourself.

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:42.357
And I think it's so silly
and people are like,

00:03:42.357 --> 00:03:43.340
"Oh, I just don't have the time."

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:45.290
I'm like, "Do you have the time to die?"

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:47.100
Like do you have the time to get cancer,

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:48.080
do you the time to do that?

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:49.850
'Cause anyway, I think
it's really important

00:03:49.850 --> 00:03:51.320
that you did speak up
and you're open about it.

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:54.940
'Cause I think like you
said, maybe men are not as

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:57.780
excited to go do that,
but it is important.

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:00.360
And speaking of health, actually,
I love how open you are.

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:03.543
You actually got a COVID
vaccine live on Today.

00:04:04.790 --> 00:04:06.360
Some think it's controversial.

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:09.450
So why was it important
to you to do on air?

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:13.840
- Look I think that, sadly,
I'm the only one (chuckles)

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:17.920
on the Today show old enough
to get the shot, I'm over 65.

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:20.970
And by the way, that ties
into the other issue.

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:23.410
You know, a lot of us, a lot
of people have been putting off

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:25.210
getting health checks,
normal health checks

00:04:25.210 --> 00:04:28.060
because of the pandemic.

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:29.220
Either because they've had to,

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:30.863
or it's just been difficult.

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:34.010
And I think there's been some reluctance

00:04:34.010 --> 00:04:35.320
for people to get it

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:37.660
and especially again, not to harp on this,

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:42.660
but people of color and
latinx folks have had issues,

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:49.110
they're in those communities,
getting the vaccine.

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:51.870
So I just wanted to go in there

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:55.467
and try to show people,
"Hey, look, this is easy.

00:04:55.467 --> 00:04:56.300
"If you can get it."

00:04:56.300 --> 00:04:59.080
Now, look, there are a
lot of issues with supply,

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:01.670
but I think it's important.

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:05.110
The only way we're gonna lick this thing

00:05:05.110 --> 00:05:08.620
is for us to all take
responsibility for ourselves

00:05:08.620 --> 00:05:09.580
and for other people.

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:11.290
So that means wearing a mask.

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:13.810
That means getting you
doing social distancing,

00:05:13.810 --> 00:05:17.750
washing hands, and getting
your COVID vaccine.

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:20.842
- And so we can get our
lives back. How about that?

00:05:20.842 --> 00:05:22.800
- That's right and in fact,

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:25.430
our parent company, Comcast NBC Universal

00:05:25.430 --> 00:05:28.200
is trying to help folks, plan your vaccine

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:31.100
so that we all get together
and you can see our loved ones.

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:35.140
So all you have to do is
go to planyourvaccine.com,

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:36.490
sign up for an interactive site.

00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:38.810
Gives you all the vaccine
information you'll need

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:41.500
in one place so that when
you get your appointment

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:43.390
you are ready to go.

00:05:43.390 --> 00:05:46.250
- Right on. You should have been a coach.

00:05:46.250 --> 00:05:47.750
You're good at it.

00:05:47.750 --> 00:05:49.890
Well, hold on you have
a Today Show interview

00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:51.710
special coming up next Monday, right?

00:05:51.710 --> 00:05:52.850
Can you talk about it?

00:05:52.850 --> 00:05:54.470
- Yeah. I'm pretty excited about this.

00:05:54.470 --> 00:05:57.600
Of the first African-American

00:05:57.600 --> 00:05:59.690
a Cardinal was named Wilton Gregory,

00:05:59.690 --> 00:06:01.090
Catholic Cardinal was named.

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:04.720
He runs the archdiocese of Washington

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:08.710
so technically he is
president Biden's pastor

00:06:08.710 --> 00:06:10.250
and I got to sit down with him

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:12.847
and he was not raised Catholic, but

00:06:14.650 --> 00:06:17.910
his family migrated up
from the South to Chicago.

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:19.560
And once he got into Catholic school

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:22.050
he knew pretty quickly
he wanted to be a priest.

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:26.130
And we talk about the
divide in our country.

00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:30.010
We talk about bias and race in our country

00:06:30.010 --> 00:06:32.150
and how even as a man of the cloth,

00:06:32.150 --> 00:06:33.560
he's had to deal with that.

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:36.720
So I really enjoyed sitting
down and talking with him

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:40.140
and we're gonna have that
coming up this coming Monday

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:41.883
on the 3rd Hour of Today.

00:06:42.896 --> 00:06:45.360
It's a really interesting conversation

00:06:46.505 --> 00:06:47.800
and I think we need,

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:50.410
we really do need to be
talking more and more

00:06:50.410 --> 00:06:51.740
with our faith leaders

00:06:51.740 --> 00:06:55.040
if we're gonna try to
unite people together.

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:56.120
- Yeah. Good gosh.

00:06:56.120 --> 00:06:58.520
About time that's what you
should name that special.

00:06:58.520 --> 00:06:59.937
About damn time (laughs).

00:07:01.390 --> 00:07:04.553
You can see Al mornings on Today on NBC.

